text:
Learning Objectives
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

identify credible open health data portals;
distinguish microdata from aggregated indicators;
complete a Data Intake Card for a public health dataset;
record basic provenance, licensing, access, and citation information;
name privacy, security, and stewardship risks that can still apply to public data;
explain why AI summaries must be checked against source documentation;
recognize when Indigenous Data Sovereignty, OCAP, or CARE principles may be relevant;
frame a dataset as the start of a KT product, not just as a file to analyze.

Open Data Portals
Open data can accelerate public health learning when the source, meaning, and limits of the data are clear. Start with reputable portals that provide documentation, terms of use, and stable links.
Recommended starter portals:


CDC/NCHS public-use survey data, such as NHANES or NHIS;

WHO Global Health Observatory for aggregated international indicators;

BC Data Catalogue for provincial open data;

Government of Canada Open Government Portal for Canadian datasets.

Each link above goes to that source’s page in the Reference: Data Sources part of this book, which also covers other sources you may use later in the course.
When you land on a dataset page, look for:

publisher or steward;
population, geography, and time coverage;
data level, such as microdata or aggregated indicators;
documentation, codebook, or methodology notes;
license or terms of use;
citation recommendation;
last updated date or access date.

Provenance
Provenance means the traceable story of where data came from and how it reached your project. A dataset is not just a filename. It has a steward, collection context, transformations, limitations, and documentation.
For every dataset, record:

original source page;
local file path if a copy is stored in the repository;
date accessed;
file format;
variables you plan to use;
known limitations or cautions;
citation or attribution language.

The Data Intake Card is the course habit that makes provenance visible.

Responsible AI
AI tools may help you draft plain-language summaries or identify questions to ask about documentation. They are not source documentation.

AI tools can also hallucinate: they can state plausible-sounding but wrong variable definitions or data facts with full confidence. They can also repeat biases from their training data. That is why Week 1 asks you to verify every definition against the source documentation, not against an AI summary.
Acceptable Week 1 AI use:

asking for a plain-language explanation of a term from a codebook;
drafting a first pass of a dataset description;
generating a checklist of questions to verify.

You can get useful AI help without uploading any data. Share the structure instead: column names, variable types, and a made-up example row. You can also share your code and your error messages. Never paste the real rows. The AI helps with code and wording; you run everything locally against the real data yourself.
Required checks:

cite the official source, not the AI output;
verify variable meanings in documentation;
include an AI-use note if AI helped draft your card;
revise AI language that overstates certainty or hides limitations.

Formats And Metadata
Common formats:

CSV: rectangular rows and columns;
JSON: nested data, common for APIs;
XLSX: spreadsheet files that may contain formatting and hidden assumptions;
RDS: R-specific saved objects;
GeoJSON or shapefiles: spatial data for maps.

Metadata explains what the file means. It may include variable definitions, units, missing-value codes, collection methods, survey design variables, and weighting instructions. A dataset without metadata is risky for analysis because column names alone rarely tell the full story.

What Students Leave With
By the end of Week 1, you should have:

one completed Data Intake Card;
one KT framing sentence;
one ethics or stewardship caution;
a clear understanding that the NHANES case study starts as a data artifact, not an app to run;
readiness to place files into a reproducible project structure in Week 2.

Approved Portals
Choose one dataset or indicator page from one of these portals:


CDC/NCHS public-use survey data, such as NHANES or NHIS;
WHO Global Health Observatory;
BC Data Catalogue;
Government of Canada Open Government Portal.

Each link goes to that source’s page in the Reference: Data Sources part of this book.
The NHANES Health Equity data spine may be used as the shared classroom example.

Completion Checklist
To be marked complete, your card must include:

stable source link or access date;
publisher or steward;
license or terms of use;
geography and time coverage;
data level and file format;
documentation or codebook location;
at least one documented variable definition;
a 1-2 sentence note on how an AI tool could misrepresent or bias that variable, such as wrong category labels, outdated coding, or overgeneralization; 
one limitation or stewardship risk;
one privacy or security note;
Indigenous Data Sovereignty relevance marked as no, yes, or unknown;
KT framing sentence;
AI-use note.

Reproducibility Check
Before submitting, check:

the link opens or the access date is recorded;
the publisher and license are named;
variable definitions come from documentation, not guesses;
file format and data level are named;
the AI-use note says what AI helped with and how the text was checked.

Control Flow: Auditing AI Decisions
Deeper dive: Control Flow.
You will not write many if/else blocks or loops by hand. But AI frequently generates them, so you need to read them.
What to do with the code in this section: Read — these are patterns to recognize in AI output; you do not need to run them. 
If/Else: The Fork in the Road

if (mean_age &gt; 50) {
  print("Older cohort")
} else {
  print("Younger cohort")
}

Audit question: Is the threshold (50) appropriate for my research question, or did the AI pick an arbitrary number?
Loops: The Treadmill

for (col in c("age", "bmi", "sbp")) {
  print(summary(my_data[[col]]))
}

Audit questions: Does the loop have a clear end condition? Could this be replaced with a simpler vectorized operation (e.g., summarise(across(...)))?






Note



In R, loops are rarely the best tool. If AI gives you a loop, ask: “Can this be done without a loop using dplyr or purrr?” The answer is usually yes.

Functions (The Toaster)
Functions take input, perform an action, and return output. Look at the snippet below
Code snippet
calculate_rectangle_area &lt;- function(length, width) {
  area &lt;- length * width
  return(area)
}

# Example usage:
calculate_rectangle_area(10, 5) # Returns 50

If / Else (The Fork in the Road)
An if/else statement lets the computer make a decision.
The program checks a condition:

If it is TRUE, it runs one block of code.
If it is FALSE, it runs another.

temperature &lt;- 5
if (temperature &gt; 10) {
  print("You can wear a t-shirt")
} else {
  print("Wear a jacket")
}

Example
for (i in 1:5) {   
    print(i) 
}

The Rule: Every time you use AI to write code, write a # comment in plain English above it explaining what you told the AI to do.
Code snippet
# I asked AI to filter for patients with BMI over 30 and count them # [AI CODE BELOW]
Below is an example of non readable code
# What is x? What is 1.05? Why are we subsetting?
f &lt;- function(x, y) {
z &lt;- x[x$a &gt; y, ]
r &lt;- sum(z$b) * 1.05
return(r)
}

res &lt;- f(df, 100)
And here is an example of readable code
library(dplyr)

# Calculate the total revenue for high-value orders including a 5% tax
calculate_total_revenue &lt;- function(order_data, minimum_price_threshold) {

  sales_tax_rate &lt;- 1.05

  total_revenue &lt;- order_data |&gt;
    filter(price &gt; minimum_price_threshold) |&gt;
    summarize(total = sum(price)) |&gt;
    pull(total)

  return(total_revenue * sales_tax_rate)
}

# Usage:
final_report_value &lt;- calculate_total_revenue(current_orders, minimum_price_threshold = 100)
Just by reading through each line a reader should understand what is going on. One does not need to comment every single line, but rather comment whenever something complex is happening on the code line below it. And as always source work which is not yours.

Learning Objectives
By the end of this week, you should be able to:

explain when R is the better default and when Python may be useful;
write a clear R dplyr pipeline for a descriptive health-data summary;
refactor repeated R code into a small function;
create and run a Python notebook in Codespaces;
load and inspect a CSV with pandas;
identify common R-to-Python translation traps;
use AI one step at a time while auditing each translated output;
document Python dependencies in requirements.txt;
use Git to commit a clean translation workflow.

Assignment 4
Complete Assignment 4: Polyglot Awareness & R Deepening.
Submit a repository link on Canvas after committing:


assignments/assignment04-polyglot/submission/polyglot-parity.qmd;

assignments/assignment04-polyglot/submission/translation.ipynb;

assignments/assignment04-polyglot/submission/helpers.py.

write a clear R dplyr pipeline for a descriptive health-data summary;
refactor a repeated summary into a small R function;
translate the same task to Python/pandas one step at a time;
compare outputs across languages using row counts, grouping levels, and summary values;
document any mismatch before deciding which code to change.

Part A: R Pipeline

Write Parts A and B in one new R script named week5.R, saved in the Week 5 folder (weeks/week05-polyglot-r-deepening/). You are not editing an existing file. For Assignment 4, the same code later moves into polyglot-parity.qmd in assignments/assignment04-polyglot/submission/.
Start with R. The goal is a descriptive, unweighted summary of BMI by income group and gender.

library(tidyverse)

nhanes &lt;- read_csv("../../examples/nhanes-equity/data/nhanes_equity_v6.csv")

r_summary &lt;- nhanes |&gt;
  filter(!is.na(BMI), !is.na(IncomeGroup), !is.na(Gender)) |&gt;
  group_by(IncomeGroup, Gender) |&gt;
  summarise(
    n = n(),
    mean_bmi = round(mean(BMI), 2),
    sd_bmi = round(sd(BMI), 2),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(IncomeGroup, Gender)

r_summary

Part B: R Function
Refactor the summary so the value and grouping variables are easy to change later.
summarise_mean_sd &lt;- function(data, value, group1, group2) {
  data |&gt;
    filter(!is.na({{ value }}), !is.na({{ group1 }}), !is.na({{ group2 }})) |&gt;
    group_by({{ group1 }}, {{ group2 }}) |&gt;
    summarise(
      n = n(),
      mean_value = round(mean({{ value }}), 2),
      sd_value = round(sd({{ value }}), 2),
      .groups = "drop"
    ) |&gt;
    arrange({{ group1 }}, {{ group2 }})
}

r_function_summary &lt;- summarise_mean_sd(nhanes, BMI, IncomeGroup, Gender)
r_function_summary
Before moving to Python, check that the function output has the same number of rows and the same grouping labels as the original pipeline.

Student Output
Submit a short note with:

the R pipeline;
the R function;
the Python translation;
a parity table showing the checks above;
two sentences explaining any mismatch or confirming that the outputs agree.

Trap 2: Indentation
R uses braces to mark code blocks. Python uses indentation.
if (age &gt;= 20) {
  "adult"
} else {
  "not in adult cohort"
}
if age &gt;= 20:
    "adult"
else:
    "not in adult cohort"
If Python indentation is inconsistent, the code may fail or run with a different meaning.

load data;
inspect rows and columns;
filter missing values;
group and summarise;
compare with the R result.

When To Use It
Use reticulate when:

a small Python library solves a specific problem better than the R ecosystem;
the rest of your report, tables, or dashboard are already in R;
you can document the Python dependency clearly.

Avoid it when:

a clean R solution already exists;
mixing languages makes the project harder to reproduce;
the Python step cannot run in a fresh Codespace.

Reproducibility Check
Before committing:

verify that helpers.py is saved in the same folder as your notebook;
rerun the notebook from a fresh kernel;
check that the relative path starts from the notebook location;
commit both files together with a message such as Add Week 5 Python helper.

Proposal Readiness Checklist
Your proposal should include:

a focused health-data question;
the dataset name and source;
a brief provenance and stewardship note;
a feasibility check showing the file can load in a Codespace;
one or two planned descriptive summaries or visualizations;
any known privacy, licensing, or access limitations.

Objectives
By the end of the week you can:

build a rerunnable ggplot2 visualization from the NHANES Health Equity CSV snapshot;
identify visual choices that can exaggerate, hide, or mislabel a descriptive result;
revise title, scale, color, grouping, and caption choices to reduce misinterpretation;
document whether a plot is weighted or unweighted, descriptive or causal, and complete-case or missingness-aware;
use AI assistance as a draft partner while checking the code and claims yourself.

Prepare a Descriptive Summary
Keep the summary intentionally simple. We are describing the prepared class dataset, not estimating a national trend.

bmi_by_income_cycle &lt;- nhanes |&gt;
  filter(
    !is.na(BMI),
    !is.na(IncomeGroup),
    !is.na(Cycle),
    Age &gt;= 20,
    Age &lt;= 80
  ) |&gt;
  group_by(Cycle, IncomeGroup) |&gt;
  summarise(
    n = n(),
    mean_bmi = mean(BMI),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) |&gt;
  filter(n &gt;= 30)

bmi_by_income_cycle













Definition: hidden small cell count



A hidden small cell count is a group with very few observations that may be hidden inside a chart or summary. Do not make strong claims from these groups. That is why the code above keeps only groups with n &gt;= 30.

Objectives
By the end of the week you can:

define and document an analysis cohort before summarizing;
report missingness for key variables rather than dropping rows silently;
create a Table 1-style descriptive summary with N, mean, and SD;
distinguish what descriptive statistics claim from what they cannot claim;
audit code and prose for correctness, reproducibility, interpretation, and stewardship errors.

What “N” alone does not tell you
A cell that says N = 47 is informative only if the reader knows:

which cohort the 47 came from (cohort definition, eda02);
how many of those 47 were excluded for missing values (missingness table, also eda02);
what the comparison group sizes are (the rest of the Table 1).

If any of those three is invisible, the N is not yet defensible.

Stewardship recap: what goes in the provenance statement
The provenance/stewardship statement you produce for A6 (and reuse in M2) should name:

the data source (prepared NHANES Health Equity classroom CSV);
the source of the source (derived from public-use NHANES files at the CDC);
the access method (cached in the repo, no internet access required for class work);
the classroom-use boundary (descriptive learning only, not population inference);
one privacy or stewardship caution (e.g., no row-level data uploaded to external AI tools).

Five sentences, written carefully once, reused everywhere.

Objectives
By the end of the week you can:

explain the difference between a static report, a Quarto dashboard-style product, and a Shiny app;
render the Quarto starter dashboard locally and adapt one audience-facing element;
apply the 1-1-1-1-1 rule to translate one Week 7 finding into a dashboard prototype;
distinguish text/UI changes (allowed for A7) from analytic changes (not allowed for A7);
test a dashboard locally and record what was checked;
apply privacy-safe publishing guidance before sharing any health-data product.

Objectives
By the end of the week you can:

identify an audience and decision context for one result;
write a claim that includes evidence and a visible limitation;
build or outline a short Quarto revealjs slide story;
cite the data source for a figure, table, or dashboard screenshot;
use peer feedback to improve clarity without inflating the claim.








Key Terms This Week




Knowledge translation (KT): turning evidence into a form that helps a specific person understand it, question it, or act on it.
Decision context: the decision, conversation, or question your audience is facing when they see your result.
Audience-facing text: writing meant for your audience to read directly — not notes for yourself or your team.
Claim-evidence-limitation: the three-part pattern used for every message in this course: what you say, what supports it, and what it cannot prove.
Plain-language takeaway: the one sentence a non-expert should remember, written without jargon.

The Week 9 Task
This week asks for one small communication product:

one audience;
one question;
one figure, table, or dashboard output;
one plain-language takeaway;
one limitation;
one source note.

That is enough. More slides are not automatically better.

What Not to Do
Avoid these common shortcuts:

turning a descriptive pattern into a causal statement;
hiding the limitation in tiny text;
showing a figure without naming the data source;
copying a dashboard screenshot that exposes row-level data;
asking AI to “make it more persuasive” without checking accuracy.

Before Submitting
Check that:

the deck or outline renders;
the main evidence is visible;
the limitation is not hidden;
the source note is present;
the AI-use note says what AI helped with and what you checked.

Reproducibility Check
Before submission:

the deck or outline source is committed;
the rendered HTML is committed;
any image paths are relative;
the evidence can be traced to A5, A6, A7, or project files;
the source note and limitation are visible;
the AI-use note is specific.

Accessibility Check
Slides should be readable in a live presentation. Check:

titles are short;
body text is large enough;
figure labels are readable;
color is not the only way to distinguish groups;
the final slide gives the audience a clear next step.

Objectives
By the end of the week you can:

structure a short Quarto report around one descriptive question;
generate one table and one figure from code;
cite a data source using a BibTeX workflow;
trace key claims to code output, citations, or limitations;
choose a private or public preview route based on privacy and stewardship checks.

Report and M3
M3 is a project update, not a final report. The draft should show:

what evidence is ready;
what still needs feedback;
how the dashboard-style product connects to the report;
what reproducibility risks remain.

Data Source
Name:

the original data source;
the version or snapshot used;
the access route;
restrictions or stewardship cautions.

Use your A6 provenance/stewardship note as the starting point.

Methods
State choices before showing results:

cohort or inclusion criteria;
variables used;
missingness handling;
whether the summary is descriptive, unweighted, weighted, predictive, or causal.

For this course, most reports should be descriptive unless the instructor explicitly approved another approach.

Results
Include at least:

one code-generated table;
one code-generated figure;
a short paragraph that states the pattern without overstating it.

Do not paste screenshots when code can generate the output.

Reproducibility and AI Use
End with enough information for a reviewer:

how the report was rendered;
where data came from;
where package/dependency notes live;
what AI helped with;
how AI-assisted content was checked.

What Counts as Evidence
Evidence can be:

a code-generated table;
a code-generated figure;
a data source citation;
a methods paragraph;
a limitation statement;
a reproducibility log.

Evidence is not:

an AI-generated sentence without verification;
a visual impression with no source;
a claim copied from a website without citation;
a dashboard interaction that cannot be reproduced.

AI-Use Check
If AI helped draft report prose, audit the generated text for:

invented sources;
stronger claims than the code supports;
causal language;
missing limitations;
mismatched variable names or statistics.

Before Moving On
If a claim cannot be grounded, do one of three things:

add the missing evidence;
soften the wording;
remove the claim.

When Public Publishing Is Safe
GitHub Pages or another public preview is appropriate only when:

the data are public and allowed to be shared;
no restricted raw data are committed;
no sensitive row-level records appear in outputs;
no private URLs, tokens, or credentials appear;
the group agrees that public sharing fits the project context;
the README explains data source and sharing limits.

If any item is uncertain, keep the preview private.







GitHub Pages sites are always public



A GitHub Pages site is public on the open internet even when the source repository is private. Turning on Pages publishes the rendered content for anyone to see. If your project must stay private, the “private equivalent” is course staff previewing in a fresh Codespace or reading the committed HTML in your private repository — not a Pages site.

GitHub Pages as an Optional Extension








Demo only — not graded



This pathway is a demonstration only. It is not graded and not required. Using it for graded work requires instructor approval first. The supported, graded pathway in this course is the Quarto workflow.


GitHub Pages is useful for public portfolios, but it is not required for every student project. Public publishing should never outrank privacy, data-use rules, or stewardship obligations.
If your group uses GitHub Pages, record:

the public URL;
the commit hash;
what was checked before publishing;
what data or outputs are intentionally excluded.

Private Preview Checklist
For the default route, record:

report path;
rendered HTML path;
repository link;
latest commit hash;
who has access;
any files intentionally excluded from public sharing.

Before Submitting

Before rendering for submission, search your code for local paths such as /Users/, C:\, Downloads, or Desktop. Replace them with project-relative paths. The Search panel (the magnifying glass icon in the left sidebar) checks every file at once.

Before committing, check the staged files. If you see raw row-level data, credentials, .RData, OS junk, or generated files that are not allowed, unstage them and check .gitignore before syncing. The full pre-commit privacy checklist is on the Private Preview page.
Check:

report renders;
table and figure are code-generated;
citations resolve;
preview route is private unless public-safe;
M3 feedback request names the decisions that still need review;
all required files are committed and synced.

Objectives
By the end of the week you can:

run a project in a fresh Codespace using only documented instructions;
log failures before repairing them;
improve README, path, dependency, citation, and output hygiene;
use M4 peer review to find correctness, reproducibility, auditability, and KT risks;
decide whether optional deployment pathways are worth pursuing after the core portfolio works.

The Core Standard

The surgery covers all of it — the analysis code, the report, the dashboard-style KT product, the README, and the repository itself. You are not revising just one piece; you are revising everything a reviewer will touch.
By the end of this week, the group should know:

whether the report renders;
whether the dashboard-style KT product opens or runs;
whether paths are relative;
whether data access is documented;
whether citations resolve;
whether AI-use notes are specific;
whether README instructions match reality.

Do Not Add New Work Unless It Reduces Risk
Good Week 11 changes:

fix a broken path;
add a missing package note;
rewrite unclear README steps;
regenerate stale outputs;
document a known limitation;
remove an unsupported claim.

Risky Week 11 changes:

replacing the dashboard pathway;
adding a new package without documenting it;
adding a new analysis that is not needed for the report;
switching to public publishing without privacy review.

NHANES Exemplar Checks
For the NHANES case study, the checks are:

load examples/nhanes-equity/data/nhanes_equity_v6.csv;
render the Week 10 report template;
confirm the README explains cached data and optional CDC retrieval;
if using the Shiny exemplar as a demo, launch it from the repository root;
if using the static dashboard demo, open weeks/week11-portfolio-surgery/static-dashboard-ojs.qmd.

The CDC rebuild and static dashboard are optional/demo pathways, not required portfolio work.

Optional Python Pathway
If the project uses Python, add a short dependency note with:

how Python is launched;
required packages;
whether a notebook, script, or Quarto Python chunk is the primary workflow;
how outputs are regenerated.

Keep the Python pathway documented, but do not add it unless it supports the core audience-facing product.

README Minimums
The project README should name:

project title and audience;
project question;
data source and access route;
folder structure;
how to render the report;
how to open or run the dashboard-style KT product;
package or environment setup;
known limitations or risks;
public/private sharing status.

Git Hygiene
Before final submission:

commit repairs with meaningful messages;
avoid committing credentials, tokens, or local cache files;
keep .gitignore aligned with the project;
sync the final commit before submitting the repository link.

Before Sending Feedback
Check that your comments are:

specific;
respectful;
tied to evidence;
prioritized;
useful before final submission.

report renders;
dashboard-style KT product opens or runs;
README explains the workflow;
data source and limitations are clear;
fresh environment test has been attempted.

Optional Python
Python may be useful for a small extension if it supports the project and runs in Codespaces. Keep it awareness-level unless the group has a clear reason and a documented environment.
If Python is included, document:

where the Python code lives;
how to run it;
which packages are required;
what outputs it creates;
how it connects to the R/Quarto core.

Public Deployment
Public deployment is optional and should follow the Week 10 rule: private preview unless public-safe.
Before public sharing, check:

no restricted data;
no credentials;
no row-level sensitive examples;
aggregate outputs only;
README explains data use and limitations.

Final Portfolio Bridge

The revision target is all of it — the analysis project, the report, the dashboard-style KT product, and the repository with its README — not just one piece.
The final portfolio is not a separate new analysis. It gathers the strongest version of:

final report source and rendered output;
dashboard-style KT product or static preview;
data provenance and stewardship documentation;
reproducibility instructions;
presentation slides or presentation summary;
final AI-use audit;
clear README and repository access route.

Your dashboard-style KT product may be submitted as a static preview; a live dashboard is not required. 
Use the Final Portfolio Checklist before submission.

Two rules make this easier:

You may revise earlier work — and you are encouraged to. If feedback on an earlier assignment showed you a better way, fix it before it goes into the portfolio. The portfolio is graded on the strongest version of your work, not the first version you submitted.
Required files are prescribed; the rest of the structure is yours. The checklist names the files every portfolio must contain. Beyond those files, your group is free to organize and present the portfolio however serves your project best.


Not sure what “strong” looks like? A weak portfolio is a pile of files the reader must decode. A strong portfolio leads with a README that answers what the project is, who it is for, what was found, and how to rerun it. See the side-by-side weak vs strong README example.

Before Submitting A10
Check:

fresh environment test attempted;
failures logged before repair;
report and KT product rerun or failure clearly explained;
README matches the tested workflow;
M4 feedback is recorded;
final risk list is honest;
required files are committed and synced.

Before Final Portfolio
After A10, focus on:

repairing remaining blockers;
aligning presentation slides with final report claims;
checking repository access;
confirming public/private sharing choice;
syncing the final commit before Canvas submission.

Common Reproducibility Contract
Every assignment must:

run or render in GitHub Codespaces;
use relative paths rather than machine-specific paths;
keep source files and rendered outputs in the requested submission folder;
commit and sync the final work before submitting the repository link on Canvas;
document packages or dependencies when adding anything beyond the course defaults.
